REFLECTION=r'''
<section class="reflection-box"><h3>AUTHOR’S REFLECTION</h3><p>Every generation believes it is living through an unprecedented change.</p><p>Perhaps every generation is partly right.</p><p>The Orakzai world I inherit contains many worlds at once.</p><p>There is the memory of elders. There is the language of the home. There are mountains, villages, and family histories. There are schools, universities, and professional careers. There are cities, migration routes, and overseas communities. There are smartphones, digital platforms, and new forms of work.</p><p>None of these worlds exists completely alone. They overlap.</p><p>The modern Orakzai therefore cannot be understood by looking only at the past or only at the present. We have to look at the connection between them.</p><p>A society becomes modern not when it forgets its roots, but when it learns how to carry those roots into institutions, education, technology, and a changing world.</p><p>The responsibility of my generation is not simply to inherit a culture. It is to understand it. To preserve what gives dignity. To question what causes harm. To learn what the future requires. And to leave the next generation something stronger than what we received.</p></section><div class="final-statement"><strong>MODERNITY IS NOT THE END OF TRADITION.<br>IT IS THE CONTINUING STORY OF HOW PEOPLE ADAPT.</strong></div>
'''

